WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.601
-You look sharp, buddy.
Welcome back to our show.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:03.634
Thank you for being here.
-It's so nice to be here

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:05.534
and not in my office
at home via Zoom.

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.601
-Yeah, Zooming, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:08.901
-We have a live audience.

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:10.200
-Actual live audience.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It's the best.

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:15.868
You have a big weekend
coming up.

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:18.601
'Cause you just finished
a run of "Cabaret."

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:19.801
-That's right, yeah.
-In London.

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:21.968
I have a photo of you
in "Cabaret."

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:23.567
Look at you.

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:24.868
Congrats on this.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.934
-That is me
under there somewhere.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:30.934
-You were nominated for
an Olivier Award on Sunday.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.334
-Yeah.
-That's fantastic.

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:33.834
-You know what, it's --
this is --

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:36.067
I played --
this looks

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:39.367
like a sort of bejeweled
haute couture Skellington.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:40.734
But actually this is --

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.467
I play the Emcee in "Cabaret,"

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:45.734
and I played it as a kid
when I was at school.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.467
So it's always been one
of those lifelong dreams,

00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:49.567
and the whole show was a dream.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:51.100
So I'm thrilled.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.067
Yeah, it's the sort of cherry on
top of the --

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.434
Christmas cake?
-Yeah, cherry on top.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:58.200
Yeah. Cherry on top, yeah.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.767
"Cabaret" is, you know --

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.434
It's a little --
It's a little dark.

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:03.067
-It's pretty dark.

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.934
-Your kids probably didn't --
didn't see it, did they?

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:08.601
-Well, my kids are like
five and four.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:13.834
And I took them to the theater
recently to see "Frozen,"

00:01:13.834 --> 00:01:15.000
which they loved.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.200
-"Frozen" is fantastic.
-It's fantastic.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.267
And they're sort of beginning
to understand that I'm an actor.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.300
And I say,
"Daddy's doing a play

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.467
and doing a musical tune.

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:25.100
Do you want to come
and see the theater?"

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.367
And they came.

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:29.467
They saw that scary costume,
and that terrified them.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.133
And so I showed them the stage.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:33.534
And I kind of, you know,
sang a little song for them.

00:01:33.534 --> 00:01:36.334
And they're like, "That's great,
Dad, but it's not 'Let It Go.'"

00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:38.634
-No, it's not "Let It Go."
It will never be "Let It Go."

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:41.434
-And more depressing is that,
like, the songs from "Cabaret"

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:42.934
are like --

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:45.234
the song that I was listening
on repeat learning it was like,

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:47.667
♪ Money makes the world
go around ♪

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.734
But it's like a really
dark sort of take on that.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:52.000
And so my kids are like,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.400
♪ Money makes the world
go round ♪

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.067
No, No, No --
-Let's put on "Frozen."

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.501
Let's put on "Frozen."
That's much better.

00:01:56.501 --> 00:01:58.501
"Frozen 2," whatever you want.
-Exactly.

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:01.100
-Your first acting role --
is this correct?

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.901
Was it "Oliver"?
-Yeah.

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.100
Yes, gosh, you did
your research.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.267
-I did a little research.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:10.100
-Who did you play in "Oliver"?
Were you Oliver?

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.167
-It's still actually
quite painful.

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.300
-Everyone has to start
somewhere.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.667
-And the other thing in "Oliver"
is like,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.834
you're either Oliver
or you're Dodger

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:18.100
or you're one of the gang.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.367
There are hundreds
of children's parts.

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:23.601
But I had the famous part
of the book boy,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:25.534
who is not in the gang,
isn't Oliver.

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:26.868
Has one line.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:27.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
-The book boy!

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.601
Oh, my God,
we love the book boy.

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:31.634
Oh, my God, yeah.

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:32.801
That's our favorite character.

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:34.434
-The favorite character.
I had one line.

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:35.934
And it went like this,

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:37.667
"Books you wanted
from the bookseller, sir."

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.100
I had to come on.
-Wow.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.334
Nailed it.

00:02:40.334 --> 00:02:42.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's how you do it.

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:44.133
-But it was a big deal for me

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:45.300
and it was
at the London Palladium,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.367
which was like
a huge theater in London.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.300
And the first night that I
went on, I delivered my line,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.267
and I just walked very,
very slowly off,

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:56.701
took about three minutes.

00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:59.000
Sort of royal wave --
the director was like,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.767
"Mate, this isn't --
You're not Oliver.

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:03.234
You're not Dodger."

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:04.501
-No, you're gonna break --
-"Get the hell off the stage."

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:05.901
-People are gonna remember
the bookseller kid.

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:07.767
-Exactly.
-"Here's your book, sir."

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:10.834
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.834 --> 00:03:14.567
-Slowly walk out.

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.234
But when you were first
starting out,

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:19.367
you had some
pretty famous roommates.

00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:21.167
-True.
-In L.A.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.934
-It's been interesting.

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:27.834
So, about -- what was it --
15 years ago when --

00:03:27.834 --> 00:03:30.200
there's this thing in England
in the winter.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.501
It rains a lot.
And in L.A.,

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:35.033
it's really sunny, and there's
this thing called pilot season.

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.934
And so, all these kind
of out-of-work

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:39.667
English actors go over to L.A.
to try and get work,

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:41.200
which we tend not to get.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.901
But what we would do is just,
like, be in the sunshine

00:03:44.901 --> 00:03:48.834
and then sort of call home
and tell our families

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.367
that we were working
incredibly hard.

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:53.033
-[ Laughs ] I get it.
It's sunny out in L.A.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:54.534
-It's sunny out in L.A.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.934
But there was this group of us,

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:57.801
and some of us lived together,
some of us didn't.

00:03:57.801 --> 00:04:02.534
And -- But we would always go
and hang out and play.

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:03.934
And none of us got jobs.

00:04:03.934 --> 00:04:07.367
And then someone would get a job
and sort of go stratospheric

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:10.133
and then the rest of us
would sit back and --

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:11.334
-"What are we doing
with our life?"

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.567
-Yeah.

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:13.701
-Play Ping-Pong.
-There was a lot of Ping-Pong.

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:14.734
-These are people
like Andrew Garfield?

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:16.467
-Andrew, very good
at Ping-Pong.

00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:18.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.834
Fierce, fierce Ping-Pong player,
Garfield.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.767
-Is he really?
-Yeah, and I just remember

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:25.467
that he did once come --
you know when you play tennis

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:29.567
and someone comes with,
like, a bag

00:04:29.567 --> 00:04:31.300
and maybe with two rackets.
-Yeah.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.167
-I just remember once playing
with Andrew and he turned up

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:36.200
with a Ping-Pong bat
in a, like, case.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.767
I was like,
he is not messing around.

00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:43.033
But he's a very good
Ping-Pong player.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:44.467
-How is Robert Pattinson?

00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:45.868
-Rob, I didn't play
Ping-Pong with.

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:47.767
Jamie Dornan I played
Ping-Pong with.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:48.868
-Jamie Dornan too?

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:50.634
I mean, this is
the house of studs.

00:04:50.634 --> 00:04:52.667
I mean, what is going on?
-Well, it's funny because --

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:54.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:56.400
There was this thing
I read the other day

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.200
because Jamie and Rob have been
promoting things recently,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.567
they were being asked
about this.

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:06.767
And I read something about like
how Jamie was sort of

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:08.300
bemoaning the fact
of how stressful

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.701
it was going to a club with Rob
because everyone

00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:13.267
would look at Rob
and no one would look at him.

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:14.767
I was like, "Dude."

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.901
-You're Jamie --
-Try being me, man.

00:05:16.901 --> 00:05:19.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:22.667
Jamie, face of Calvin Klein,

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.367
billboards in New York,
sort of six-pack.

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:27.934
I was like, "Dude, let's not."
-Please, yeah.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:29.534
Give me a break.
-Cry me a river.

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:31.234
-A river, yeah.
Also --

00:05:31.234 --> 00:05:32.834
I read something
that Robert Pattinson was saying

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:35.033
because he was promoting
"The Batman."

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.701
And he was saying
he would go to auditions

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:39.534
and sometimes
you're in the audition

00:05:39.534 --> 00:05:41.734
and there's someone in the room
ahead of you auditioning

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.300
and either get applause
or get a big reaction

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.767
and it makes you nervous

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:47.133
because you're like,
"I got to follow that?"

00:05:47.133 --> 00:05:49.167
And the door would open,
and you would come out.

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:52.334
And he'd be like, "Oh, wow,
you killed it again, buddy.

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.300
Congrats."

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.334
-He's not doing
too badly, is he?

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:55.767
-No, he's not.
He's doing okay.

00:05:55.767 --> 00:05:57.234
-The thing about Rob
and it was interesting,

00:05:57.234 --> 00:06:01.634
'cause this whole bunch of us
and there was Charlie Cox,

00:06:01.634 --> 00:06:03.033
Tommy Sturridge --

00:06:03.033 --> 00:06:06.467
A load of us who all have
remained really good buddies.

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:09.968
But each of us had our own
kind of audition technique.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:13.667
And Rob and Tom's was always
like whatever the scene

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:17.934
appears to be on the page,
you do the complete opposite.

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:19.467
And so, I would be
sitting outside,

00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.667
and I thought it was
a quiet, intimate scene

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:25.334
and I just hear sort of shrieks
inside the room.

00:06:25.334 --> 00:06:27.234
I was always -- We're basically
all desperately

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:28.567
trying to find
our way to like...

00:06:28.567 --> 00:06:31.100
-Of course.
-...stand out from the crowd.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.968
-He would just open
the door like,

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:34.434
"I'm not going to get that one."

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:35.901
[ Laughter ]
-But good shrieks.

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:37.300
-Yeah, good shrieks, he tried.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.133
I want to talk about the new
film "Fantastic Beasts:

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.567
The Secrets of Dumbledore."
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.701
-Can I quickly just tell
one more story?

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:46.934
Because when we made
"Fantastic Beasts,"

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:50.868
it was at the same studio
that Rob was making "Batman."

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:53.968
-Oh, yeah.
-And we shared a gym

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:56.934
because my character
and my body is so buff.

00:06:56.934 --> 00:06:58.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:00.767
Anyway, one day
I went to the gym,

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:03.901
and I walked in
and there was this guy

00:07:03.901 --> 00:07:06.567
with, like, a high ponytail.

00:07:06.567 --> 00:07:09.367
Massive, doing the most
extraordinary stunts

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:11.267
I've ever seen
with his stunt director.

00:07:11.267 --> 00:07:13.567
And it was that slightly
embarrassing thing

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:14.934
when you're a skinny runt

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.667
and you go into a gym
and there's someone --

00:07:16.667 --> 00:07:19.033
and this is, like, the same gym
that Jason Momoa goes to.

00:07:19.033 --> 00:07:21.801
You're quite used to --
-I would walk the opposite way.

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:24.667
-And I walk in and this guy
goes, "hi" and I go "hi"

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:26.400
and then I just sort of
sit there watching him

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.634
do these incredible things

00:07:27.634 --> 00:07:32.501
while I sort of weakly do
a sit-up in the corner.

00:07:32.501 --> 00:07:35.167
-You're like waving a wand
in the corner.

00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:37.000
[ Laughter ]
-Gotta get my wand on.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.267
-[ Grunting ]
You're like, "I'm good."

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.334
-It was that.
It was that.

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:46.868
Anyway, after 40 minutes
of doing this,

00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:49.801
I sort of --
not 40 minutes. 10 minutes.

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:51.567
I sort of scuttle off,

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:53.901
and as I'm going,
this guy goes, "Bye, Eddie."

00:07:53.901 --> 00:07:55.801
And I look back, and it was Rob.

00:07:55.801 --> 00:07:59.534
And he was so physically
changed and he had a man bun.

00:07:59.534 --> 00:08:04.467
And what I had seen him
do was so dumbfounding.

00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:07.667
It was -- He completely
transformed himself.

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:09.367
I felt very lucky to see him
because "Batman"

00:08:09.367 --> 00:08:12.133
is so brilliant
and his work in it.

00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:15.567
I felt really lucky
to see his commitment.

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:16.567
-Isn't that fun to see?

00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:19.801
I like seeing my friends
at work.

00:08:19.801 --> 00:08:21.167
-This was at 5:00
in the morning.

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.200
He's like sort of --
I was doing my wand lifting

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:30.367
and he was doing ferocious,
hard-core fights.

00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:31.934
-He went for it. Yeah.
He did look different.

00:08:31.934 --> 00:08:33.801
-It was very impressive.

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:35.000
-Speaking of impressive,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.634
this is
another "Fantastic Beasts:

00:08:36.634 --> 00:08:39.501
The Secrets of Dumbledore."

00:08:39.501 --> 00:08:41.934
I love watching you in any role
that you play.

00:08:41.934 --> 00:08:43.968
You always dive right in.

00:08:43.968 --> 00:08:47.534
For this third installment,
it's a giant film.

00:08:47.534 --> 00:08:49.200
It's a giant deal.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.868
Is there anything you could
say about it or --

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:53.367
'cause I don't want
to give away secrets.

00:08:53.367 --> 00:08:57.367
-What I love about this one
is it feels like

00:08:57.367 --> 00:08:59.834
it's a sort
of wizarding heist movie.

00:08:59.834 --> 00:09:03.133
It's kind of like "Oceans 11."

00:09:03.133 --> 00:09:05.300
-But for young family.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.467
-In a wizarding, like, world.
-Yes, I love that.

00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:09.534
I love that. Yes.

00:09:09.534 --> 00:09:10.601
-Is that a way to try and --

00:09:10.601 --> 00:09:12.734
-Yeah, it's a perfect way
to sell it.

00:09:12.734 --> 00:09:15.501
-And Jude Law,
who plays Dumbledore,

00:09:15.501 --> 00:09:19.634
is basically George Clooney
and I'm Brad Pitt.

00:09:19.634 --> 00:09:22.467
[ Laughter ]
-And that's -- So he's George,

00:09:22.467 --> 00:09:25.200
you're Brad and that's --
-Can't you see the similarity?

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.167
-Yes, exactly right.

00:09:27.167 --> 00:09:28.567
You know what?

00:09:28.567 --> 00:09:29.968
I was going to ask you --
every time you're here --

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:31.400
I don't mean to bother you.
You don't have to do this.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.634
But you always either
bring me something

00:09:32.634 --> 00:09:34.100
from the set
that you've learned

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.400
or a magic trick
you showed me once

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.334
or something you had
to physically do in the movie.

00:09:39.334 --> 00:09:40.601
Do you have
any embarrassing things?

00:09:40.601 --> 00:09:41.734
-It's worse than that.

00:09:41.734 --> 00:09:44.367
Because I feel that
on the first movie,

00:09:44.367 --> 00:09:49.734
they made me do a mating dance
with basically a rhino.

00:09:49.734 --> 00:09:52.133
[ Laughter ]
And then I came

